  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/6D4749">Definition <span data-tag="6D4749">6.5.1.10</span>.</a>connective!$n$-connective objectconnective!$n$-connective morphism Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism in an $\infty $-topos $\calX $, and let $0 \leq n \leq \infty $. We will say that $f$ is <em>$n$-connective</em> if it is an effective epimorphism and $\pi _ k(f) = \ast $ for $0 \leq k < n$. We shall say that the object $X$ is <em>$n$-connective</em> if $f: X \rightarrow 1_{\calX }$ is $n$-connective, where $1_{\calX }$ denotes the final object of $\calX $. By convention, we will say that every morphism $f$ in $\calX $ is $(-1)$-connective. </p>
